-- DICT-SEMANTICS §8 I3: "EVIDENCE TRAVELS, IT IS NOT RE-DERIVED. When a
-- constrained binding crosses a module boundary, its callers discharge its
-- residual predicates from THEIR ambient `P` (§4 `var`); THE BINDING ITSELF DOES
-- NOT RE-RESOLVE THEM. This keeps a single derivation per predicate-occurrence
-- and preserves C2/C3 across modules."
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): the only `impl Sz Coin` is declared HERE, strictly
-- DOWNSTREAM of `prov.twice` -- `prov` does not import `main` and cannot see it.
-- So `twice` provably could not have resolved this evidence itself; the dict is
-- supplied by this call site. `twice (Coin 21)` = 21 + 21 = 42.
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all ACCEPT and print 42. CONFORMANT.
--
-- The downstream placement is the whole design of this fixture: with the impl in
-- `prov.mdk` the row would pass under an implementation that re-resolves at the
-- definition site, and would prove nothing about I3.
import prov.{Sz, sz, twice}

data Coin = Coin Int

impl Sz Coin where
  sz (Coin n) = n

main = println (twice (Coin 21))
